  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain overspreads the region Sunday afternoon and \nlasts through the night. \n\nSome decent cooling tonight as we've managed to hold off the clouds \nthrough 2AM but moisture streaming out ahead of the approaching \nsystem will mean a partly to mostly cloudy start to the day by \nsunrise. Regional radar shows returns as far north as central NY but \nthe heavier rain actually reaching the ground confined to PA for \nnow. This precipitation shield will continue to expand north and \neast through Sunday overspreading western CT/MA by late \nmorning/early afternoon and eastern MA by mid afternoon to early \nevening. Continuing to expect a widespread moderate rainfall as \nanomolous moisture originating from the Gulf makes its way overhead \nwith isentropic forcing for ascent as well as a 30-45 kt LLJ. The \ncore of this jet fortunately remains aloft and offshore to the south \nso while winds will be breezy, the main impact from this system is \n12-18 hours of rainfall. When all is said and done we expect a quick \n0.75\"-1\" of rainfall, as high as 1.5\" along the immediate south \ncoast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry Monday then a few rounds of showers, first later \nTuesday into Tuesday night, then around Friday into Saturday.  \n\nBrief respite from the rain comes Monday as most all of southern New \nEngland should be done with the rain by 8am with drier air and \nclearing skies following shortly behind on N/NW flow. After a brief \nwarm-up Monday into the mid 60s and low 70s (CT Valley) 925mb temps \nquickly drop back to the 9-11C range on Tuesday (similar to Sunday). \nThis means the return of highs in the low 60s. The brief drop in \ntemps precedes a subtle shortwave passing to our north late Tuesday. \nReturn flow ahead of the trough brings a surge in temps and \ndewpoints Tuesday night/Wednesday making Wednesday the warmest day \nof the week. There is potential for lower or even upper 70s, though \nconfidence is low at this point that we'll get that warm. EPS \nensembles have a >50% chance of >70F for much of the region while \nthe GEFS odds are even better. A cold front then drops through \nsometime Wednesday bringing a much cooler end to the week. This \nfrontal system brings rain around late Tuesday into early Wednesday \nthen our next system doesn't arrive until around Friday.",
